Vaccines have led to the eradication of smallpox, a disease that once killed millions, demonstrating their effectiveness in preventing deadly illnesses.
Extensive scientific research and real-world data consistently show that vaccines reduce the incidence of diseases like measles and hepatitis B, saving countless lives.
The success of global vaccination programs, such as those for polio, highlights how vaccines can significantly reduce disease spread and improve public health.
Vaccines work by training the immune system to recognize and fight pathogens, a well-established biological mechanism supported by decades of medical research.
The overwhelming consensus among medical professionals and health organizations worldwide underscores the safety and efficacy of vaccines in preventing disease.